@synopsis
**yumed** **--config** *path* [ **--validate** ]
**yumed** **--config** *path* **--status**
**yumed** **--version**
**yumed** **--completion** **bash**
**yumed** **--help**
@end

## DESCRIPTION

**yumed** is the native YUME Transport Protocol 1 daemon. It reads a schema-1
server configuration, serves its configured static cover site, authenticates
clients and carries traffic for declared services through direct TCP/UDP
adapters, module programs or a Linux managed TUN. Each service needs a matching adapter; application-owned named
services use the embedding interface. The daemon checks destination policy
before routing and checks every resolved address before connecting.

A module adapter's program runs as a supervised child process that receives
its service's streams over a private UNIX socket below **TMPDIR**. The daemon
restarts it with backoff when it exits. See **docs/MODULES.md**.

With a **cluster** section the daemon is a member of its operator's
cluster and keeps an authenticated link to every other member it holds
secrets for. The links carry no traffic yet.

The daemon is not yet qualified for production, and routes over several
servers are not yet available. Check **docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md** before
deployment.

## OPTIONS

@include shared/native_cli.part

The status option asks the running daemon of the same configuration for its
state.

@options
@opt **--status**
@cli spell: --status
@cli help: Print the running daemon's listeners and cluster links

Connect to the control socket that **control.socket** names, print the
running daemon's state and exit. The report names the listeners, the number
of client sessions and, for a cluster member, the list's serial and expiry
and each peer's links: the outbound link's state, how long it has lasted or
when the next attempt starts and its last failure, and the peer's inbound
sessions. The exit status is 2 when the configuration names no control
socket and 1 when no daemon answers on it. **--status** takes no option
besides **--config**.
@end

@include shared/native_configuration.part

## CLUSTER

A server's **cluster** object names five files: **operator_key**, the
operator's composite public key, **list**, the cluster list, **signature**,
its composite signature, **peers**, the peer store, and **state**, where the
daemon saves the highest list serial it has loaded. The list names at
most 64 servers with their addresses and public keys, and the operator signs
it with a key that stays off the servers. The daemon refuses to start with a
list whose signature does not verify, whose **not_after** has passed or that
does not name the daemon itself. The peer store gives each peer an outbound
PSK, an inbound PSK and the peer's admission key. Every PSK differs from
every other PSK, admission key and client PSK, and a peer is never also a
client or administrator. All five files are owner-only like the other
credential files. The daemon creates and replaces **state** itself, so its
directory must exist and be writable by the daemon, for example
**/var/lib/yume/cluster-state.json** under the packaged unit, whose
**/etc/yume** is read-only. It refuses a list with a lower serial than the
saved one, after a restart too, and a state file of another cluster.
**yume-setup** writes all of it: **cluster-init**,
**cluster-add**, **cluster-remove** and **cluster-sign**. The format is in
**docs/protocol/CLUSTER_1.md**.

Once its listeners accept, the daemon keeps one outbound link to each peer, a
YTP/1 session like a client's that offers no service, and reconnects with
backoff when it ends. Each peer's own link arrives like a client's session:
the peer may hold two at once and may open no stream. When the list's
**not_after** passes, the daemon closes its links and ends the peers'
sessions until a reload loads a newer list.

## COVER AND ADMISSION

The daemon loads an operator-provided static site with explicit index and
not-found files. It has no built-in fallback site or reverse-proxy backend.
Ordinary cover requests can use HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 over TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3.
YTP/1 promotion requires TLS 1.3, HTTP/2 and authenticated admission bound to
the connection's TLS exporter. Failed admission remains cover traffic.

## PRIVILEGE

Run as an unprivileged user. Prefer a high listen port or grant only the
low-port binding capability needed for port 443. The native daemon does not
implement automatic privilege dropping or host firewall configuration. Managed
TUN requires **CAP_NET_ADMIN** and access to **/dev/net/tun** in its network
namespace; configured per-link DNS also requires systemd-resolved authorization.
Module programs run as the daemon's user without capabilities.

## SIGNALS

**SIGINT** and **SIGTERM** stop the daemon after its sessions close, followed
by removal of owned TUN networking. Modules receive **SIGTERM**, then
**SIGKILL** after five seconds.
**SIGHUP** reloads the authorized and admin key stores and the server's
composite and ML-KEM keys without dropping other clients. Every later session
authenticates against them, including one on a connection accepted before
the reload. Sessions of removed
identities, or beyond a lowered per-identity **max_sessions**, end. Established
sessions apply changed grants to their next stream and a changed **weight** to
their next transfer. A cluster member also reads its list, routes view and
peer store again, and serves the new view on **yume.routes**. A link whose peer and inputs are unchanged keeps running, a changed
peer's link is replaced, a removed peer's link closes and a new peer gets
one. A newer serial is saved before anything switches. An invalid store, a
list naming another operator, a list with a lower serial or a state file
that cannot be written is refused and the previous credentials stay in
force. TLS material, the
admission key and egress lists are read only at startup, and a changed
admission key is refused.
**SIGPIPE** is ignored.

## CONTROL SOCKET

@include shared/native_control.part

The daemon's answer holds its listeners, the number of client sessions
and, in a cluster, the list's serial and expiry, each peer's links and the
circuits this server carries, counted per link and never per pair of
neighbours.

## EXAMPLES

Validate an existing server kit, then start its configured listeners:

@code bash
yumed --config /path/to/server.json --validate
yumed --config /path/to/server.json
@end

The separately installed **yume-setup** and **yume-doctor** tools
provision and inspect schema-1 kits. Their **--help** output describes their
arguments.

